WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.573
A model cannot produce every possible observation vector. A constant model, for example, can only produce

00:00:06.573 --> 00:00:12.947
outputs whose entries are all equal. Fitting chooses the point in this small output space closest

00:00:12.947 --> 00:00:14.540
to the observed targets.

00:00:15.067 --> 00:00:21.314
The model predicts the same value for every observation. Its representable outputs lie on the line

00:00:21.314 --> 00:00:23.267
spanned by the all-ones vector.

00:00:23.767 --> 00:00:29.697
Differentiating the sum of squared residuals and setting the derivative to zero gives the sample mean.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:35.731
If one target increases by three, how much will the fitted constant move? Pause here and

00:00:35.731 --> 00:00:37.460
make a prediction before continuing.

00:00:38.567 --> 00:00:46.520
These are prediction-minus-observation residuals. Individual residuals can be nonzero at the best fit. For our starting

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:50.497
inputs, Fitted constant: 2. Squared residual sum: 2.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:57.257
The residual is orthogonal to the only model direction. It cannot be reduced further by shifting

00:00:57.257 --> 00:00:58.430
every prediction together.

00:00:58.933 --> 00:01:03.821
Inspect the residual pattern as well as the fitted parameters. A best fit can still be

00:01:03.821 --> 00:01:05.653
a poor model of the data.

